    questions: [
      { type: 'conceptual', question: 'What are the four necessary conditions for deadlock?', answer: 'Mutual exclusion (resources cannot be shared), Hold and wait (processes hold resources while requesting more), No preemption (resources cannot be forcibly taken), Circular wait (a chain of processes each waiting for the next).', explanation: 'All four must hold simultaneously for deadlock to occur. Breaking any one prevents deadlock.', commonMistakes: 'Listing only three conditions or confusing mutual exclusion with atomicity.', tip: 'Remember: Mutually Holding No Circularly. Or think: MHN-C.' },
      { type: 'numerical', question: 'Two threads access shared data 5000 times/sec each. The critical section window is 2ms. What is the race probability per access?', answer: 'P = 1 - e^(-5000 x 0.002) = 1 - e^(-10) = 1 - 0.0000454 = 0.99995, essentially 100%.', explanation: 'With 5000 accesses per second and a 2ms window, overlaps are virtually guaranteed on every access.', commonMistakes: 'Using the wrong time unit (seconds vs milliseconds).', tip: 'Convert everything to the same units before plugging into the formula.' },
      { type: 'application', question: 'When should you use a spin lock instead of a mutex?', answer: 'When the critical section is very short (a few instructions) and contention is low. Spin locks avoid the overhead of context switching to the OS scheduler.', explanation: 'Spin locks busy-wait, wasting CPU cycles. For long critical sections, this wastes more than a context switch would cost.', commonMistakes: 'Using spin locks for long critical sections, wasting CPU on busy-waiting.', tip: 'Spin for nanoseconds, sleep for microseconds.' },
      { type: 'problemSolving', question: 'Design a dining philosophers solution that guarantees no deadlock and no starvation.', answer: 'Assign a total ordering to forks. Each philosopher picks up the lower-numbered fork first, then the higher-numbered one. This breaks circular wait. Add a limit: at most N-1 philosophers may sit at the table simultaneously, preventing excess demand.', explanation: 'The ordering breaks circular wait. The limit prevents all philosophers from competing at once, ensuring at least one can always eat.', commonMistakes: 'Only breaking circular wait without addressing starvation.', tip: 'Ordering prevents deadlock. Fair scheduling prevents starvation.' }
    ],
